
## v0.7.3 — conditional RF history, incumbent shortcut, and pass stability

### Pass 12 — avoid reinforcing bad learned routes

#### 1. Learned LWR/NLWR history could dominate candidate ranking even when AUTO was bad

**Observed:** Real-network v0.7.2 data showed nodes where a current learned route received the highest RF score because it matched LWR/NLWR, yet benchmarked hundreds of milliseconds slower than a lower-scored one-hop route.

**Fix:** Learned-route evidence now starts at weak weight. After current/AUTO baselines are measured, untouched generated candidates are re-ranked. A clean AUTO result at or below the one-hop quality thresholds enables full learned-history weight; a poor AUTO result sets learned-history weight to zero. Direct controller-link RSSI remains useful regardless. No route is hard-filtered by RF telemetry.

#### 2. A known-good application pin still paid the full exhaustive cost

**Observed:** The known-good FLiRS lock route remained ~45–50 ms across repeated runs but still benchmarked every generated candidate.

**Fix:** A clean incumbent at or below 60 ms median / 150 ms worst can stop expansion after three distinct RF-ranked challenger paths if the incumbent still wins using the configured minimum-improvement hysteresis. A meaningful challenger prevents the shortcut and the existing incumbent-vs-challenger confirmation remains mandatory before replacement.

#### 3. Multi-pass output required manual interpretation

**Problem:** Compact pass summaries exposed winners, but the caller still had to manually decide whether AUTO/direct differences represented physical route changes or merely representation/speed changes.

**Fix:** Whole-network responses now include `route_stability`. Per-node classification distinguishes exact candidate stability, physical-path stability, stable AUTO policy with a changing learned physical path, true instability, single-pass insufficiency, and incomplete data. AUTO physical paths are normalized from the starting effective learned route for each pass.

### v0.7.3 safety invariants

- Whole-network Apply remains hard-disabled.
- RF/history data only changes candidate order; it never removes topology-valid candidates.
- Poor AUTO performance cannot increase learned-route preference.
- Incumbent adaptive stopping requires a clean, bounded-latency incumbent and three challenger paths.
- Existing replacement confirmation and fail-safe restoration semantics are unchanged.
